# SOLID Principles

**SOLID** is an acronym for five object-oriented design principles that help developers write software that is more **maintainable**, **understandable**, and **flexible**. Following them leads to better design, easier scaling, and simpler testing.

---

## 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

> A class should have only one reason to change — it should have exactly one responsibility.

**Benefit:** Improves readability and maintainability by reducing complexity, and makes the code easier to refactor.

---

## 2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

> Software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension but closed for modification.

**Benefit:** Lets the system adapt to new requirements by adding code rather than changing existing code, which lowers the risk of introducing bugs.

---

## 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

> Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without affecting the correctness of the program.

**Benefit:** Enables safe polymorphism, making code more reusable and reducing duplicated logic.

---

## 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

> Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use.

**Benefit:** Prevents "fat" interfaces and promotes a decoupled, modular design that is easier to maintain and change.

---

## 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

> High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.
> Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions.

**Benefit:** Promotes loose coupling, making the system easier to change and maintain. It also improves testability by allowing dependencies to be replaced with mocks or stubs.

---

## Summary

| Letter | Principle | Core Idea |
|:------:|-----------|-----------|
| **S** | Single Responsibility | One class, one reason to change |
| **O** | Open/Closed | Extend behavior without modifying existing code |
| **L** | Liskov Substitution | Subclasses must be usable in place of their base class |
| **I** | Interface Segregation | Many small, specific interfaces over one large one |
| **D** | Dependency Inversion | Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations |
